{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Three days.</b> A trebling of days — טרציינ\"א בְּלַעַ\"ז; and likewise שִׁבְעַת יָמִים everywhere — a שֶׁבַע of days." "<b>And there was darkness, gloom: three days.</b> A darkness of gloom — for three days they did not see, each man, his fellow. And another three days, darkness doubled upon this — they could not rise, each man, from under it: one sitting could not stand, and one standing could not sit. And why did He bring darkness upon them? Because there were in Israel in that generation wicked people who did not want to leave, and they died during the three days of darkness, so that the Egyptians would not see their downfall and say, 'They too are being stricken like us.' And further, so that Israel searched and saw their vessels — and when they went out and asked them and they said, 'We have nothing,' he would say to him, 'I saw it in your house, and it is in such-and-such a place' (Shemot Rabbah)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The la'az is Old French trezien/trejor, 'a period of three days'; Rashi explains the construct form with a numeric suffix."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:51:26.140121761Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}